`ssrTransform` rewrites imports to `__vite_ssr_import_N__`, skipping identifiers it thinks are shadowed by a local. Its scope walker didn't know `switch` introduces a block scope, so a `let` declared directly in a case (no braces) was registered on the enclosing **function** — which makes every same-named import reference in that function look shadowed:

```js
import { createObjectProperty as o } from './ast'

export function buildProps(node) {
  const marker = () => o('ref_for')
  switch (o(node.type)) {
    case 1:
      let o = node.value
      console.log(o)
      break
  }
  return o(marker)
}
```

Before this PR, all three `o` references to the import are left untouched — but the `import` statement they refer to has been replaced, so each one throws `ReferenceError: o is not defined` at runtime:

```js
// ...
const __vite_ssr_import_0__ = await __vite_ssr_import__("./ast", {"importedNames":["createObjectProperty"]});

function buildProps(node) {
  const marker = () => o('ref_for');
  switch (o(node.type)) {
    case 1:
      let o = node.value;
      console.log(o);
      break
  };
  return o(marker)
}
```

With this PR, they resolve to the import, while `console.log(o)` correctly stays the case-local:

```js
// ...
const __vite_ssr_import_0__ = await __vite_ssr_import__("./ast", {"importedNames":["createObjectProperty"]});

function buildProps(node) {
  const marker = () => (0,__vite_ssr_import_0__.createObjectProperty)('ref_for');
  switch ((0,__vite_ssr_import_0__.createObjectProperty)(node.type)) {
    case 1:
      let o = node.value;
      console.log(o);
      break
  };
  return (0,__vite_ssr_import_0__.createObjectProperty)(marker)
}
```

The fix makes `switch` a block scope, so case declarations register on the switch node — the single scope all cases share. The discriminant is excluded from it: `switch (o(node.type))` is evaluated before the case block exists, so its `o` is still the import.

The same bug applied to `function` and `class` declarations in a case, which this fixes too. Modules are strict, so both are block-scoped to the case block with no Annex B hoisting — a same-named import used after the switch was being left bare in exactly the same way:

```js
import { f, C } from './m'

function run(v) {
  switch (v) {
    case 1:
      function f() {}
      class C {}
      break
  }
  return [f(), new C()]   // before: left bare → ReferenceError
}
```

Found this while implementing [oxc-project/monitor-oxc#191](https://github.com/oxc-project/monitor-oxc/pull/191), currently I have skipped the failed [test](https://github.com/oxc-project/monitor-oxc/blob/c8b37f5114f518932f741be6b90fc22860257d62/runtime-repos.json#L40-L45)

The function in the dist was like
```js
function importMetaResolve(specifier) {
  if (import.meta.resolve) return import.meta.resolve(specifier);
  return new URL(specifier, new URL("../../../src/node/plugins/importAnalysisBuild.ts", import.meta.url)).href;
}
```
but this should be
```js
function importMetaResolve(specifier) {
  if (import.meta.resolve) return import.meta.resolve(specifier);
  return new URL(specifier, import.meta.url).href;
}
```

This is because of https://github.com/vitejs/vite/blob/578ffb80d46940f3b99cd96ed609f8b3a0ac5ede/packages/vite/rolldown.config.ts#L293.

This only affects users without `import.meta.resolve` support and if the app is using relative base.

